# Story: an evening of making Arbor one's own, with commands, which is also everything the settings window does: each of
# its controls runs one of these. A wider gap, a slip that is refused, a shortcut moved, a rule by the app's name, an
# app left alone, a switch on a key.
# Written by the executor swarm (F12); for the documenter to review.
# Reference: T72, A17. Window ids start at 101; `open Safari` has the bundle id com.example.safari.
# As the first build shipped it: the whole map of shortcuts, of which nine ship now (22-nine-keys.sim).
set shortcuts all
open Safari
open Mail
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
zoom 101
expect fills 101
# A wider gap applies at once: `fills` counts the gap in use.
set gap 40
expect ok
expect fills 101
# A slip changes nothing and says why.
set gap 500
expect fail
expect fills 101
set gpa 10
expect fail
reset gap
expect fills 101
show-all
# The Desktop's shortcut moves to other keys, written as menus show them; the old keys are free again.
expect shortcut ctrl+opt+d on
bind ⌃⌥X desktop
expect ok
unbind ctrl+opt+d
expect shortcut ctrl+opt+x on
expect shortcut ctrl+opt+d off
# A key that would no longer type is refused.
bind x desktop
expect fail
reset bindings
expect shortcut ctrl+opt+d on
expect shortcut ctrl+opt+x off
# A rule for an app by the name people know it by, since it has a window open.
rule add Mail dock left
expect ok
open Mail
expect docked 103
# An app nobody has heard of is not taken for a bundle id.
rule add Keynote dock left
expect fail
# Leave an app alone, and take it back.
set dont-arrange add Safari
expect ok
expect count docked 2
set dont-arrange remove safari
expect ok
# A switch on a key: the focus ring off and on.
focus 102
expect ring 102
set focusRing toggle
expect ring none
set focusRing toggle
expect ring 102
# Everything back, except what took longest to make.
set gap 30
reset all
expect ok
open Mail
expect docked 104
